+/**
+ * # CategoryDOSAudioPump
+ *
+ * **DOSKUTSU local addition (patches/SDL/0067, never upstreamed).** This
+ * header exports a single SDL3-DOS audio-backend helper: an engine-callable
+ * "audio-mid-gap pump" that runs one `SDL_PlaybackAudioThreadIterate` cycle
+ * from the calling (engine main) thread, refilling the SB16 audio ring
+ * ahead of the cooperative-scheduler audio thread's normal cadence.
+ *
+ * ## Why this exists
+ *
+ * The 486-class campaign showed that the SFX stutter on the slow Intel
+ * DX2-66 / DX2-50 cells is gap-driven, not rate-driven (flush-instr #75,
+ * 486-CLASS-CAMPAIGN-FINDINGS.md sec.16.3): individual render gaps in the
+ * engine's per-flip wall-clock occasionally exceed the audio ring depth
+ * (~372 ms in the production config), and the cooperative scheduler only
+ * runs the audio thread when the engine yields. During one long render
+ * flip the ring drains and underruns regardless of average rate.
+ *
+ * The audio-render-decoupling design (`docs/internal/AUDIO-MID-GAP-SERVICE-
+ * DESIGN.md`) calls for engine call-sites that mid-gap-service the audio
+ * ring -- the engine calls `SDL_DOSAudioPump()` at strategic points (Leg A:
+ * stage-load freads; Leg B: mid-Renderer-phase seams) so the audio thread
+ * gets serviced even during a long single-flip render gap.
+ *
+ * The pump is an SDL3 audio-backend helper rather than an engine-internal
+ * mechanism because the audio iterate sequence (GetDeviceBuf -> SDL3 core
+ * mix loop -> SDL3_mixer MixerCallback -> per-track pull -> PlayDevice ->
+ * SB16 ring write) lives on the SDL side, not in the engine.
+ *
+ * ## How it works
+ *
+ * `SDL_DOSAudioPump()` runs ONE `SDL_PlaybackAudioThreadIterate` cycle
+ * on the currently-open DOS audio device and returns a bool indicating
+ * whether the caller may call again to top up the ring further. That
+ * one Iterate runs exactly what the audio thread's own loop runs each
+ * cycle: it grabs the device lock, the mixer produces one staging
+ * buffer's worth of mixed audio (firing SDL3_mixer's MixerCallback
+ * and the per-track get-callbacks synchronously),
+ * `DOSSOUNDBLASTER_PlayDevice` writes the buffer into the SB16 ring
+ * (with the SDL/0054 Shape A silence-detect skip if applicable), and
+ * the lock is released. After Iterate returns, the pump reads the
+ * ring's room snapshot (same formula as WaitDevice's own room check)
+ * and returns true iff the ring is not yet full.
+ *
+ * Caller pattern: `while (SDL_DOSAudioPump()) { }`. The engine wraps
+ * each Leg A `load_stage` seam in this loop, topping up the audio ring
+ * to full per seam. v1 (one-iterate-per-call, void) gave ~10% coverage
+ * over 400-1400 ms load_stage windows -- inadequate for the gap-driven
+ * underrun. v2 (while-loop, bool) tops up the ring to its full depth
+ * per seam, eliminating the single-seam coverage gap.
+ *
+ * The audio thread, when it next runs, finds the ring partially or
+ * fully refilled and either waits less in WaitDevice or skips the
+ * mix entirely on that cycle.
+ *
+ * Net effect: the engine pre-emptively services the audio ring during
+ * what would otherwise be a single-yield-point gap, so the ring does
+ * not drain to underrun during long render flips.
+ *
+ * ## Safety properties (REQUIRED -- the engine call-sites depend on these)
+ *
+ * Three properties hold and MUST keep holding for the engine's mid-
+ * Renderer-phase call-sites to remain safe (flush-instr design doc sec.3.1):
+ *
+ * 1. **Does NOT touch the SDL renderer.** No `SDL_Render*` calls, no
+ *    surface lifecycle, no DAC programming, no framebuffer flip, no
+ *    palette / cursor mutation, no drawcall queue access. The entire
+ *    Iterate path stays in `vendor/SDL/src/audio/` and the DOS audio
+ *    backend (`vendor/SDL/src/audio/dos/`).
+ *
+ * 2. **Does NOT run any SDL event handlers.** No internal `SDL_PumpEvents`
+ *    call. No event-watch dispatch from the audio thread (the one event-
+ *    adjacent path, `SDL_AudioDeviceDisconnected`, fires only on failure
+ *    and defers via `SDL_RunOnMainThread`; it never synchronously
+ *    dispatches event handlers, and under the DOS backend's normal
+ *    returns it does not fire at all).
+ *
+ * 3. **Does NOT re-enter engine code via callback (beyond scalar slot
+ *    bookkeeping).** The one synchronous engine-callback re-entry that
+ *    can fire inside the iterate is SDL3_mixer's
+ *    `MIX_SetTrackStoppedCallback` chain, which lands in Pixtone's
+ *    `mySfxCallback` -> `pxtSoundDone`. `pxtSoundDone` is two scalar
+ *    writes (`_sound_fx[slot].channel = -1` + `--g_pixtone_active_count`)
+ *    -- the "marks a slot free" carve-out that the safety surface
+ *    explicitly allows. No other engine state is touched, no renderer
+ *    is reached, no event pump is run, no `SDL_LockMutex` is taken on
+ *    any engine-managed mutex.
+ *
+ * If any of (1)/(2)/(3) ever changes (e.g. a future SDL3_mixer change
+ * adds a synchronous callback that touches more), this header must be
+ * updated and the engine call-sites re-audited.
+ *
+ * ## Lifecycle + idempotence
+ *
+ * - Safe to call at any time. If the audio device has not been opened
+ *   (or has been closed), the call is a harmless no-op (early return).
+ * - Idempotent in the sense that calling it twice in quick succession
+ *   simply runs two iterate cycles -- the audio thread's own loop
+ *   tolerates the ring being refilled ahead of its schedule (it waits
+ *   in WaitDevice for ring space when full; the mix-skip silence-detect
+ *   path also handles double-fill safely).
+ * - Must NOT be called from an interrupt context (the iterate path
+ *   takes locks and may yield via the cooperative scheduler).
+ * - Callable from the engine main thread; not designed to be called
+ *   from the audio thread itself (that would self-deadlock on the
+ *   device lock).
+ *
+ * ## Engine-side gate
+ *
+ * The pump export itself is **unconditional** -- the export always
+ * attempts one iterate when called. The decision whether to call it
+ * lives engine-side and is gated by the engine's reading of
+ * `SDL_HINT_DOSKUTSU_AUDIO_MIDGAP_PUMP` -- **default-ON, strict-"0"
+ * disables** (killswitch shape, same as SDL/0066's SHUTDOWN_PARK).
+ * The engine reads this hint on its first `SDL_DOSAudioPump()`
+ * invocation and caches the decision; absent the killswitch, the
+ * engine calls `SDL_DOSAudioPump` in a top-up loop at each of the 6
+ * `load_stage` seams (per nxengine-evo/0162), iterating until the
+ * ring is full (the v2 `while (SDL_DOSAudioPump()) { }` caller
+ * pattern -- see the function's return-value contract below). With
+ * the killswitch set to "0", the engine short-circuits before
+ * `SDL_DOSAudioPump` -- the SDL-side export is unaffected (it stays
+ * unconditional) but no engine call-site invokes it. Set "0" only to
+ * A/B-confirm the Leg A pump against the pre-0162 freeze on real
+ * DX2-66 hardware.
+ *
+ * ## Real-HW gate
+ *
+ * Per `memory/dosbox_not_behavioral_proxy_for_io.md`, DOSBox-X cannot
+ * reliably reproduce the cooperative-scheduler timing that the gap-
+ * driven SFX stutter depends on. A DOSBox-X smoke gate can verify
+ * structural correctness (the export is present, the engine call-sites
+ * compile, the game still hits title) but only a real-DX2-66 iter
+ * confirms whether the pump materially closes underrun gaps. The
+ * matching Leg A real-HW iter contract carries the cells.
+ *
+ * Like `SDL_dosvesa_directaccess.h` and `SDL_dosaudio_shutdown.h`, this
+ * header is a workspace-local doskutsu addition. SDL/0067 hunks and
+ * these declarations are not contributed back to libsdl-org/SDL.
+ */
+
+#ifndef SDL_dosaudio_pump_h_
+#define SDL_dosaudio_pump_h_
+
+#include <SDL3/SDL_stdinc.h>
+
+#include <SDL3/SDL_begin_code.h>
+/* Set up for C function definitions, even when using C++ */
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+extern "C" {
+#endif
+
+/**
+ * Run one SDL_PlaybackAudioThreadIterate cycle on the currently-open
+ * SDL3-DOS audio device, from the calling (engine main) thread, and
+ * return whether the caller should call again to top up the ring
+ * further.
+ *
+ * This refills the SB16 audio ring ahead of the cooperative-scheduler
+ * audio thread's normal cadence -- pre-empts a potential underrun
+ * during a long single-flip render gap. The engine calls this from
+ * Leg A (stage-load freads; v2 wraps the call in
+ * `while (SDL_DOSAudioPump()) { }` per nxengine-evo/0162, iterating
+ * until the ring is full per seam) and, later, Leg B (mid-Renderer-
+ * phase seams) per the audio-render-decoupling design.
+ *
+ * Return value:
+ * - `true`  = the iterate did meaningful work AND the ring is not
+ *             yet full. The caller MAY call again to top up further.
+ * - `false` = either the iterate was a no-op (TryLock+timeout
+ *             backstop tripped per SDL/0068; device->shutdown
+ *             observed; NULL device), or the defense-in-depth iter
+ *             cap fired (32 consecutive true-returns in one
+ *             call-sequence), or the ring is now full (the audio
+ *             thread will drain naturally; further pumps waste CPU).
+ *             The caller's `while`-loop exits.
+ *
+ * Safety: this call honors the three properties documented in the
+ * file-head comment above -- it does NOT touch the SDL renderer, does
+ * NOT run any SDL event handlers, and does NOT re-enter engine code
+ * beyond scalar slot bookkeeping. The v2 while-loop pattern repeats
+ * the Iterate path N times serially; each individual iteration
+ * honors all three properties; the loop introduces no new touch
+ * points. The engine call-sites depend on these properties.
+ *
+ * Behavior:
+ * - NULL device (not opened or already closed) -> returns false; the
+ *   internal cap counter resets so the next call-sequence (after the
+ *   caller's `while`-loop exits) starts fresh.
+ * - Cap exceeded (>32 consecutive true-returns in one call-sequence
+ *   -- defense-in-depth against caller-side bugs) -> log a one-line
+ *   "cap tripped" diagnostic on the SDL log channel, reset counter,
+ *   return false.
+ * - Otherwise: acquire `device->lock` (via the SDL/0068 TryLock+
+ *   timeout backstop when active, or via blocking `SDL_LockMutex`
+ *   when the SDL/0068 killswitch is set), run one
+ *   `SDL_PlaybackAudioThreadIterate` cycle (recursive lock; we hold
+ *   the outer), read the ring's room snapshot inside the outer lock
+ *   (same formula as WaitDevice's own room check for predicate-
+ *   boundary consistency), release the outer lock, and return true
+ *   iff Iterate ran AND the ring has room for another chunk.
+ * - Must be called from the engine main thread, not from an interrupt
+ *   context, not from the audio thread itself.
+ *
+ * \returns true if the caller may call again to top up further; false
+ *          if the caller's `while`-loop should exit (no work was done,
+ *          or the ring is full, or the iter cap tripped).
+ *
+ * \threadsafety The call grabs and releases the SDL audio device lock;
+ *               it serializes with the audio thread's own iterate
+ *               cycles via that lock. Safe to call from the engine
+ *               main thread at any point that does not already hold
+ *               the device lock.
+ *
+ * \since This function is available since SDL/0067 (doskutsu
+ *        workspace-local; never upstreamed). v2 (this signature)
+ *        ships in the SDL/0067 amendment alongside SDL/0068's
+ *        TryLock+timeout backstop and nxengine-evo/0162's
+ *        `while (SDL_DOSAudioPump()) { }` call-site pattern.
+ *
+ * \sa SDL_DOSAudioBeginShutdown
+ */
+extern SDL_DECLSPEC bool SDLCALL SDL_DOSAudioPump(void);
+
+#ifdef __cplusplus
+}
+#endif
+#include <SDL3/SDL_close_code.h>
+
+#endif /* SDL_dosaudio_pump_h_ */
